\end{reflist}  \index{certainty=<certainty>|oddindex}\index{cert=@cert!<certainty>|oddindex}\index{type=@type!<certainty>|oddindex}\index{locus=@locus!<certainty>|oddindex}\index{assertedValue=@assertedValue!<certainty>|oddindex}\index{given=@given!<certainty>|oddindex}\index{degree=@degree!<certainty>|oddindex}
\begin{reflist}
\item[]\begin{specHead}{TEI.certainty}{<certainty> }indicates the degree of certainty associated with some aspect of the text markup. [\xref{https://www.tei-c.org/release/doc/tei-p5-doc/en/html/CE.html\#CECECE}{22.1.2. Structured Indications of Uncertainty}]\end{specHead} 
    \item[{Module}]
  certainty
    \item[{Attributes}]
  \begin{itemize}
\item \hyperref[TEI.att.scoping]{att.scoping}\begin{itemize}
\item \textit{@target}
\item \textit{@match}
\end{itemize} 
\item \hyperref[TEI.att.global]{att.global}\begin{itemize}
\item @xml:id
\item @n
\item @xml:lang
\item @xml:base
\item @xml:space
\end{itemize} 
\item \hyperref[TEI.att.global.rendition]{att.global.rendition}\begin{itemize}
\item @rend
\item @style
\item @rendition
\end{itemize} 
\item \hyperref[TEI.att.global.linking]{att.global.linking}\begin{itemize}
\item @corresp
\item @synch
\item @sameAs
\item @copyOf
\item @next
\item @prev
\item @exclude
\item @select
\end{itemize} 
\item \hyperref[TEI.att.global.analytic]{att.global.analytic}\begin{itemize}
\item @ana
\end{itemize} 
\item \hyperref[TEI.att.global.facs]{att.global.facs}\begin{itemize}
\item @facs
\end{itemize} 
\item \hyperref[TEI.att.global.change]{att.global.change}\begin{itemize}
\item @change
\end{itemize} 
\item \hyperref[TEI.att.global.responsibility]{att.global.responsibility}\begin{itemize}
\item \unusedattribute{cert}
\item @resp
\end{itemize} 
\item \hyperref[TEI.att.global.source]{att.global.source}\begin{itemize}
\item @source
\end{itemize} 
\item \hyperref[TEI.att.typed]{att.typed}\begin{itemize}
\item \unusedattribute{type}
\item @subtype
\end{itemize} 
\end{itemize} \hfil\\[-10pt]\begin{sansreflist}
    \item[@cert]
  (certainty) signifies the degree of certainty associated with the object pointed to by the \hyperref[TEI.certainty]{<certainty>} element.
\begin{reflist}
    \item[{Derived from}]
  \hyperref[TEI.att.global.responsibility]{att.global.responsibility}
    \item[{Status}]
  Optional
    \item[{Datatype}]
  \hyperref[TEI.teidata.certainty]{teidata.certainty}
\end{reflist}  
    \item[@type]
  characterizes the element in some sense, using any convenient classification scheme or typology; sample categorization of annotations of uncertainty might use following values:
\begin{reflist}
    \item[{Derived from}]
  \hyperref[TEI.att.typed]{att.typed}
    \item[{Status}]
  Optional
    \item[{Datatype}]
  \hyperref[TEI.teidata.enumerated]{teidata.enumerated}
\end{reflist}  
    \item[@locus]
  indicates more exactly the aspect concerning which certainty is being expressed: specifically, whether the markup is correctly located, whether the correct element or attribute name has been used, or whether the content of the element or attribute is correct, etc.
\begin{reflist}
    \item[{Status}]
  Required
    \item[{Datatype}]
  \hyperref[TEI.teidata.enumerated]{teidata.enumerated}
    \item[{Legal values are:}]
  \begin{description}

\item[{name}]uncertainty concerns whether the name of the element or attribute used is correctly applied.
\item[{start}]uncertainty concerns whether the start of the element is correctly identified.
\item[{end}]uncertainty concerns whether the end of the element is correctly identified.
\item[{location}]uncertainty concerns both the start and the end of the element.
\item[{value}]uncertainty concerns the content (for an element) or the value (for an attribute)
\end{description} 
\end{reflist}  
    \item[@assertedValue]
  provides an alternative value for the aspect of the markup in question—an alternative generic identifier, transcription, or attribute value, or the identifier of an \hyperref[TEI.anchor]{<anchor>} element (to indicate an alternative starting or ending location). If an {\itshape assertedValue} is given, the confidence level specified by {\itshape degree} applies to the alternative markup specified by {\itshape assertedValue}; if none is given, it applies to the markup in the text.
\begin{reflist}
    \item[{Status}]
  Recommended
    \item[{Datatype}]
  \hyperref[TEI.teidata.word]{teidata.word}
    \item[]\exampleFont {<\textbf{certainty}\hspace*{6pt}{assertedValue}="{placeName}"\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}{cert}="{low}"\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}{locus}="{name}"\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}{target}="{\#ESX}">}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{desc}>}It is unlikely, but possible, that this refers to the place\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} rather than the person.{</\textbf{desc}>}\mbox{}\newline 
{</\textbf{certainty}>}
    \item[{Note}]
  \par
This attribute makes it possible to indicate the degree of confidence in a specific alternative to some aspect of the markup. In the first example above the encoder is expressing the likelihood (.2) that the generic identifier should be \hyperref[TEI.placeName]{<placeName>} rather than \hyperref[TEI.persName]{<persName>}, which is the coded element.
\end{reflist}  
    \item[@given]
  indicates one or more element(s) characterizing the conditions which are assumed in the assignment of a degree of confidence.
\begin{reflist}
    \item[{Status}]
  Recommended
    \item[{Datatype}]
  1–∞ occurrences of \hyperref[TEI.teidata.pointer]{teidata.pointer} separated by whitespace
    \item[{Note}]
  \par
The envisioned typical value of this attribute would be the identifier of another \hyperref[TEI.certainty]{<certainty>} element or a list of such identifiers. It may thus be possible to construct probability networks by chaining \hyperref[TEI.certainty]{<certainty>} elements together. Such networks would ultimately be grounded in unconditional \hyperref[TEI.certainty]{<certainty>} elements (with no value for {\itshape given}). The semantics of this chaining would be understood in this way: if a \hyperref[TEI.certainty]{<certainty>} element is specified, via a reference, as the assumption, then it is not the attribution of uncertainty that is the assumption, but rather the assertion itself. For instance, in the example above, the first \hyperref[TEI.certainty]{<certainty>} element indicates that the confidence in the identification of the new scribe as \textit{msm}. The second indicates the degree of confidence that \textit{Essex} is a personal name, given that the new scribe is \textit{msm}. Note that the given in the second \hyperref[TEI.certainty]{<certainty>} element is not the assertion that the likelihood that msm is the new scribe is 0.6, but simply the assertion that msm is the new scribe; this is a recommended convention to facilitate building networks.\par
The ambitious encoder may wish to attempt complex networks or probability assertions, experimenting with references to other elements or prose assertions, and deploying feature structure connectives such as \hyperref[TEI.alt]{<alt>}, \hyperref[TEI.join]{<join>}, and \hyperref[TEI.note]{<note>}. However, we do not believe that the \hyperref[TEI.certainty]{<certainty>} element gives, at this time, a comprehensive ambiguity-free system for indicating certainty.
\end{reflist}  
    \item[@degree]
  indicates the degree of confidence assigned to the aspect of the markup named by the {\itshape locus} attribute.
