    \item[{Example}]
  \leavevmode\bgroup\exampleFont \begin{shaded}\noindent\mbox{}{<\textbf{listObject}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{object}\hspace*{6pt}{xml:id}="{AlfredJewel}">}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{objectIdentifier}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{country}>}United Kingdom{</\textbf{country}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{region}>}Oxfordshire{</\textbf{region}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{settlement}>}Oxford{</\textbf{settlement}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{institution}>}University of Oxford{</\textbf{institution}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{repository}>}Ashmolean Museum{</\textbf{repository}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{collection}>}English Treasures{</\textbf{collection}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{idno}\hspace*{6pt}{type}="{ashmolean}">}AN1836p.135.371{</\textbf{idno}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{idno}\hspace*{6pt}{type}="{wikipedia}">}https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred\textunderscore Jewel{</\textbf{idno}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{objectName}>}Alfred Jewel{</\textbf{objectName}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{</\textbf{objectIdentifier}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{physDesc}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{p}>} The Alfred Jewel is about 6.4 cm in length and is made of combination of filigreed {<\textbf{material}>}gold{</\textbf{material}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} surrounding a polished teardrop shaped piece of transparent {<\textbf{material}>}quartz{</\textbf{material}>}. Underneath the rock crystal\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} is a cloisonné enamel image of a man with ecclesiastical symbols. The sides of the jewel holding the crystal in\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} place contain an openwork inscription saying "AELFRED MEC HEHT GEWYRCAN", meaning 'Alfred ordered me made'. {</\textbf{p}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{</\textbf{physDesc}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{history}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{origin}>}It is generally accepted that the Alfred Jewel dates from the {<\textbf{origDate}>}late 9th Century{</\textbf{origDate}>} and was\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} most likely made in {<\textbf{origPlace}>}England{</\textbf{origPlace}>}. {</\textbf{origin}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{provenance}\hspace*{6pt}{when}="{1693}">}The jewel was discovered in 1693 at Petherton Park, North Petherton in the English county of\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} Somerset, on land owned by Sir Thomas Wroth. North Petherton is about 8 miles away from Athelney, where King Alfred\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} founded a monastery. {</\textbf{provenance}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{provenance}\hspace*{6pt}{when}="{1698}">}A description of the Alfred Jewel was first published in 1698, in the Philosophical\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} Transactions of the Royal Society.{</\textbf{provenance}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{acquisition}>} It was bequeathed to Oxford University by Colonel Nathaniel Palmer (c. 1661-1718) and today is in the\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} Ashmolean Museum in Oxford. {</\textbf{acquisition}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{</\textbf{history}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}{</\textbf{object}>}\mbox{}\newline 
{</\textbf{listObject}>}\end{shaded}\egroup 


