    \item[msdescription: ]
   \hyperref[TEI.additional]{additional} \hyperref[TEI.history]{history} \hyperref[TEI.msContents]{msContents} \hyperref[TEI.msDesc]{msDesc} \hyperref[TEI.physDesc]{physDesc}\par 
    \item[namesdates: ]
   \hyperref[TEI.object]{object} \hyperref[TEI.objectIdentifier]{objectIdentifier}
    \item[{Note}]
  \par
The \hyperref[TEI.object]{<object>} element is a recent addition to the TEI P5 Guidelines as of version 3.5.0 and as such may be more prone to further revision in the next few releases as its use develops. This may be particularly evident where its contents have been borrowed from \hyperref[TEI.msDesc]{<msDesc>} and have yet to be generalized from their use in the context of manuscript descriptions.\par
The \hyperref[TEI.object]{<object>} element may be used for describing any object, text-bearing or not, though where the textuality of the object is the primary concern or a collection is mostly composed of manuscripts, encoders may prefer the \hyperref[TEI.msDesc]{<msDesc>} element (a more specific form of \hyperref[TEI.object]{<object>}) which may be used not only to describe manuscripts but any form of text-bearing objects such as early printed books.
    \item[{Example}]
  \leavevmode\bgroup\exampleFont \begin{shaded}\noindent\mbox{}{<\textbf{listObject}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{object}\hspace*{6pt}{xml:id}="{Alfred\textunderscore Jewel}">}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{objectIdentifier}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{country}>}United Kingdom{</\textbf{country}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{region}>}Oxfordshire{</\textbf{region}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{settlement}>}Oxford{</\textbf{settlement}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{institution}>}University of Oxford{</\textbf{institution}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{repository}>}Ashmolean Museum{</\textbf{repository}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{collection}>}English Treasures{</\textbf{collection}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{idno}\hspace*{6pt}{type}="{ashmolean}">}AN1836p.135.371{</\textbf{idno}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{idno}\hspace*{6pt}{type}="{wikipedia}">}https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred\textunderscore Jewel{</\textbf{idno}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{objectName}>}Alfred Jewel{</\textbf{objectName}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{</\textbf{objectIdentifier}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{physDesc}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{p}>} The Alfred Jewel is about 6.4 cm in length and is made of combination of filigreed {<\textbf{material}>}gold{</\textbf{material}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} surrounding a polished teardrop shaped piece of transparent {<\textbf{material}>}quartz{</\textbf{material}>}. Underneath the rock\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} crystal is a cloisonné enamel image of a man with ecclesiastical symbols. The sides of the jewel holding the\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} crystal in place contain an openwork inscription saying "AELFRED MEC HEHT GEWYRCAN", meaning 'Alfred ordered\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} me made'. {</\textbf{p}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{</\textbf{physDesc}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{history}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{origin}>}It is generally accepted that the Alfred Jewel dates from the {<\textbf{origDate}>}late 9th Century{</\textbf{origDate}>} and\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} was most likely made in {<\textbf{origPlace}>}England{</\textbf{origPlace}>}. {</\textbf{origin}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{provenance}\hspace*{6pt}{when}="{1693}">}The jewel was discovered in 1693 at Petherton Park, North Petherton in the English\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} county of Somerset, on land owned by Sir Thomas Wroth. North Petherton is about 8 miles away from Athelney,\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} where King Alfred founded a monastery. {</\textbf{provenance}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{provenance}\hspace*{6pt}{when}="{1698}">}A description of the Alfred Jewel was first published in 1698, in the Philosophical\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} Transactions of the Royal Society.{</\textbf{provenance}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{acquisition}>} It was bequeathed to Oxford University by Colonel Nathaniel Palmer (c. 1661-1718) and today is in\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} the Ashmolean Museum in Oxford. {</\textbf{acquisition}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{</\textbf{history}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}{</\textbf{object}>}\mbox{}\newline 
{</\textbf{listObject}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\textit{<!-- Elsewhere in document -->}\mbox{}\newline 
{<\textbf{p}>} The {<\textbf{objectName}\hspace*{6pt}{ref}="{\#MinsterLovellJewel}">}Minster Lovell Jewel{</\textbf{objectName}>} is probably the most similar to the\mbox{}\newline 
{<\textbf{objectName}\hspace*{6pt}{ref}="{\#Alfred\textunderscore Jewel}">}Alfred Jewel{</\textbf{objectName}>} and was found in {<\textbf{placeName}\hspace*{6pt}{ref}="{\#MinsterLovell}">}Minster\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} Lovell{</\textbf{placeName}>} in {<\textbf{placeName}\hspace*{6pt}{ref}="{\#Oxfordshire}">}Oxfordshire{</\textbf{placeName}>} and is kept at the {<\textbf{orgName}\hspace*{6pt}{ref}="{\#AshmoleanMuseum}">}Ashmolean Museum{</\textbf{orgName}>}.\mbox{}\newline 
{</\textbf{p}>}\end{shaded}\egroup 


    \item[{Example}]
  \leavevmode\bgroup\exampleFont \begin{shaded}\noindent\mbox{}{<\textbf{listObject}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{object}\hspace*{6pt}{xml:id}="{MaskOfTutankhamun}">}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{objectIdentifier}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{objectName}\hspace*{6pt}{xml:lang}="{en}">}Mask of Tutankhamun{</\textbf{objectName}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{idno}\hspace*{6pt}{type}="{carter}">}256a{</\textbf{idno}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{idno}\hspace*{6pt}{type}="{JournalD'Entrée}">}60672{</\textbf{idno}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{idno}\hspace*{6pt}{type}="{exhibition}">}220{</\textbf{idno}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{institution}>}Museum of Egyptian Antiquities{</\textbf{institution}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{address}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{street}>}15 Meret Basha{</\textbf{street}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{district}>}Ismailia{</\textbf{district}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{settlement}>}Cairo{</\textbf{settlement}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{country}>}Egypt{</\textbf{country}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{location}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{geo}>}30.047778 31.233333{</\textbf{geo}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{</\textbf{location}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{</\textbf{address}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{</\textbf{objectIdentifier}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{msContents}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{p}>}The back and shoulders of the mask is inscribed with a protective spell in Egyptian hieroglyphs formed of ten\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} vertical and horizontal lines. This spell first appeared on masks in the Middle Kingdom at least 500 years\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} before Tutankhamun, and comes from chapter 151 of the {<\textbf{title}>}Book of the Dead{</\textbf{title}>}.{</\textbf{p}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{</\textbf{msContents}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{physDesc}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{p}>} The mask of Tutankhamun is 54cm x 39.3cm x 49cm. It is constructed from two layers of high-karat gold that\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} varies in thickness from 1.5-3mm. It weighs approximately 10.23kg and x-ray crystallography shows that it is\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} composed of two alloys of gold with a lighter 18.4 karat shade being used for the face and neck while a heavier\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} 22.5 karat gold was used for the rest of the mask.{</\textbf{p}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{p}>}In the mask Tutankhamun wears a nemes headcloth which has the royal insignia of a cobra (Wadjet) and vulture\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} (Nekhbet) on it. These are thought respectively to symbolize Tutankhamun's rule of both Lower Egypt and Upper\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} Egypt. His ears are pierced for earrings. The mask has rich inlays of coloured glass and gemstones, including\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} lapis lazuli surrounding the eye and eyebrows, quartz for the eyes, obsidian for the pupils. The broad collar is\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} made up of carnelian, feldspar, turquoise, amazonite, faience and other stones.{</\textbf{p}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{</\textbf{physDesc}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{history}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{origin}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{p}>}The mask of Tutankhamun was created in {<\textbf{origPlace}>}Egypt{</\textbf{origPlace}>} around {<\textbf{origDate}\hspace*{6pt}{type}="{circa}"\hspace*{6pt}{when}="{-1323}">}1323 BC{</\textbf{origDate}>}. It is a death mask of the 18th-dynasty ancient Egyptian Pharaoh Tutankhamun\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} who reigned 1332–1323 BC. {</\textbf{p}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{</\textbf{origin}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{provenance}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{p}>}The mask of Tutankhamun was found in his burial chamber at Theban Necropolis in the Valley of the Kings in\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} 1922. On 28 October 1925 the excavation team led by English archaeologist Howard Carter opened the heavy\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} sarcophagus and three coffins and were the first people in around 3,250 years to see the mask of Tutankhamun.\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} Carter wrote in his diary: {<\textbf{quote}>} The pins removed, the lid was raised. The penultimate scene was disclosed –\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} a very neatly wrapped mummy of the young king, with golden mask of sad but tranquil expression, symbolizing\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} Osiris … the mask bears that god's attributes, but the likeness is that of Tut.Ankh.Amen – placid and\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} beautiful, with the same features as we find upon his statues and coffins. The mask has fallen slightly\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} back, thus its gaze is straight up to the heavens. {</\textbf{quote}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{</\textbf{p}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{</\textbf{provenance}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{acquisition}>} In December 1925, the mask was removed from the tomb, placed in a crate and transported 635\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} kilometres (395 mi) to the Egyptian Museum in Cairo, where it remains on public display. {</\textbf{acquisition}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{</\textbf{history}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{additional}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{adminInfo}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{custodialHist}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{custEvent}\hspace*{6pt}{when}="{1944}">}When it was discovered in 1925, the 2.5kg narrow gold beard was no longer attached to\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} the mask and was reattached to the chin by use of a wooden dowel in 1944.{</\textbf{custEvent}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{custEvent}\hspace*{6pt}{when}="{2014-08}">} In August 2014 when the mask was removed from its display case for cleaning, the\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} beard fell off again. Those working in the museum unadvisedly used a quick-drying epoxy to attempt to fix\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} it, but left the beard off-centre. {</\textbf{custEvent}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{<\textbf{custEvent}\hspace*{6pt}{when}="{2015-01}">}The damage was noticed and repaired in January 2015 by a German-Egyptian team who\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt} used beeswax, a material known to be used as adhesives by the ancient Egyptians.{</\textbf{custEvent}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{</\textbf{custodialHist}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{</\textbf{adminInfo}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}\hspace*{6pt}{</\textbf{additional}>}\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}{</\textbf{object}>}\mbox{}\newline 
{</\textbf{listObject}>}\end{shaded}\egroup 


    \item[{Content model}]
  \mbox{}\hfill\\[-10pt]\begin{Verbatim}[fontsize=\small]
