    \item[@evidence]
  (evidence) indicates support for the listed personal pronouns.
\begin{reflist}
    \item[{Status}]
  Recommended
    \item[{Datatype}]
  \hyperref[TEI.teidata.enumerated]{teidata.enumerated}
    \item[{Suggested values include:}]
  \begin{description}

\item[{conjecture}](conjecture) The given value was selected based on assumptions by someone besides the person to whom this pronoun applies. As a result, the value may be erroneous.
\item[{selfIdentification}](self identification) The given value has been explicitly stated or confirmed by the person to whom this pronoun applies.
\item[{trustedThirdParty}](trusted third party) The given value has been supplied by another individual trusted by the encoder to know the preferences of the person to whom this pronoun applies.
\end{description} 
\end{reflist}  
    \item[@calendar]
  indicates one or more systems or calendars to which the date represented by the content of this element belongs.
\begin{reflist}
    \item[\xref{http://www.tei-c.org/Activities/Council/Working/tcw27.xml}{Deprecated}]
  will be removed on 2024-11-11
    \item[{Status}]
  Optional
    \item[{Datatype}]
  1–∞ occurrences of \hyperref[TEI.teidata.pointer]{teidata.pointer} separated by whitespace
    \item[{Schematron}]
   <sch:rule context="tei:*[@calendar]"> <sch:assert test="string-length( normalize-space(.) ) gt 0"> @calendar indicates one or more  systems or calendars to which the date represented by the content of this element belongs,  but this <sch:name/> element has no textual content.</sch:assert> </sch:rule>
\end{reflist}  
    \item[@value]
  (value) supplies a regularized value for personal pronouns.
\begin{reflist}
    \item[{Status}]
  Recommended
    \item[{Datatype}]
  1–∞ occurrences of \hyperref[TEI.teidata.enumerated]{teidata.enumerated} separated by whitespace
    \item[{Sample values include:}]
  \begin{description}

\item[{e}](e) e/eirs
\item[{he}](he) he/him/his
\item[{she}](she) she/her/hers
\item[{they}](they) they/them/theirs
\end{description} 
    \item[{Note}]
  \par
The sample values list shown here is intended to be reflective of English usage. There is nothing restricting users from defining lists reflective of other languages, e.g. elle, il, and ils.
\end{reflist}  
\end{sansreflist}  
