    \item[@type]
  (type) may be used to indicate whether the offset passage is spoken or thought, or to characterize it more finely.
\begin{reflist}
    \item[{Status}]
  Optional
    \item[{Datatype}]
  \hyperref[TEI.teidata.enumerated]{teidata.enumerated}
    \item[{Suggested values include:}]
  \begin{description}

\item[{spoken}](spoken) representation of speech
\item[{thought}](thought) representation of thought, e.g. internal monologue
\item[{written}](written) quotation from a written source
\item[{soCalled}](so called) authorial distance
\item[{foreign}](foreign) foreign words
\item[{distinct}](distinct) linguistically distinct
\item[{term}]technical term
\item[{emph}](emph) rhetorically emphasized
\item[{mentioned}](mentioned) refering to itself, not its normal referent
\end{description} 
\end{reflist}  
\end{sansreflist}  
