    \item[@target]
  supplies a pointer to the base being glossed by this ruby text.
\begin{reflist}
    \item[{Status}]
  Optional
    \item[{Datatype}]
  \hyperref[TEI.teidata.pointer]{teidata.pointer}
    \item[{Schematron}]
  Enforce that \textit{either} {\itshape target} or both {\itshape from} and {\itshape to} (or none) are used, but not {\itshape target} in combination with either {\itshape from} or {\itshape to}. <sch:rule context="tei:rt/@target"> <sch:report test="../@from | ../@to">When target= is present, neither from= nor to= should be.</sch:report> </sch:rule>
    \item[{Note}]
  \par
Should point to a single \hyperref[TEI.rb]{<rb>} or an element that is inside an \hyperref[TEI.rb]{<rb>}. To refer to multiple elements or text nodes at once use {\itshape from} and {\itshape to}.
\end{reflist}  
    \item[@from]
  points to the starting point of the span of text being glossed by this ruby text.
\begin{reflist}
    \item[{Status}]
  Optional
    \item[{Datatype}]
  \hyperref[TEI.teidata.pointer]{teidata.pointer}
    \item[{Schematron}]
  Enforce the presence of {\itshape to} iff there is a {\itshape from}. <sch:rule context="tei:rt/@from"> <sch:assert test="../@to">When from= is present, the to= attribute of <sch:name/> is required.</sch:assert> </sch:rule>
\end{reflist}  
    \item[@to]
  points to the ending point of the span of text being glossed.
\begin{reflist}
