    \item[@scheme]
  supplies the name of the character set system from which this property is drawn.
\begin{reflist}
    \item[{Status}]
  Optional
    \item[{Datatype}]
  \hyperref[TEI.teidata.enumerated]{teidata.enumerated}
    \item[{Sample values include:}]
  \begin{description}

\item[{Unicode}](Unicode) ISO 10646{[Default] }
\item[{Bridwell}](E. Nelson Bridwell) Original character set developed by E. Nelson Bridwell as described by Al Turniansky, in use from the 1950s to 1985.
\item[{Brewer}](Georg Brewer) Developed by Georg Brewer, with a look similar to the Byrne glyphs. Like the Byrne set, this is not a true character set, but rather a set of alternate glyphs. 
\item[{Doyle}](Darren Doyle) Glyph set (in some cases associated with multiple characters) developed by Darren Doyle as part of a comprehensive version of the language created in part for an invented language classs at UT Austin in 2006. 
\item[{Schreyer}](Christine Schreyer) Character set (without actual codepoints) of 153 characters developed, along with pronunciation rules, roughly 300 words, and a grammar, developed by Christine Schreyer developed in 2012 for Warner Brothers. 
\end{description} 
\end{reflist}  
\end{sansreflist}  
    \item[{Example}]
  In this example a definition for the Unicode property  {\name Decomposition Mapping} is provided.\leavevmode\bgroup\exampleFont \begin{shaded}\noindent\mbox{}{<\textbf{unicodeProp}\hspace*{6pt}{name}="{Decomposition\textunderscore Mapping}"\mbox{}\newline 
\hspace*{6pt}{value}="{circle}"/>}\end{shaded}\egroup 


    \item[{Note}]
  \par
All name-only attributes need an xs:boolean attribute value inside {\itshape value}.
\end{reflist}  
